**Right column (top-right, mid-right, bot-right) has the HIGHEST
|Δytox| values in the image (4.68, 5.50, 6.04 mean per tile) — exactly
the region with the W44-178 -7 SSIM2 deficit.** The W44-178 max-abs RGB
in the right column is small (0.0074-0.008) precisely because the
content there is smooth — but on smooth content, even a ±5 unit cmap
divergence integrated across an entire 64×64 tile produces sub-pixel
chroma shifts that SSIM2 amplifies.

Right-column tx=15 pass-2 per-tile detail:
- Every single one of the 16 tiles has nonzero Δytox (we say 0, cjxl says +4/+5/+6/+7).
- ty=15 has Δytob = -10 (we say -24, cjxl says -14) — the worst single
  tile in the right column.

Methodology
-----------
1. Added env-gated dump module `vardct/w44_182_dump.rs` (95 LOC, env
   `JXL_W44_182_DUMP_CFL=<dir>`, zero overhead when unset). Records
   per-tile (tx, ty, pass, ytox, ytob).
2. Added 2 dump-call sites in `vardct/chroma_from_luma.rs` (pass=1 at
   end of `compute_cfl_map`, pass=2 at end of `refine_cfl_map`).
3. Encoded clic_097cb426 e7 d=5 with ours, captured 512 dump rows
   (256 tiles × 2 passes).
4. Patched libjxl `lib/jxl/enc_chroma_from_luma.cc` with a small (32
   LOC) env-gated cmap dump (full diff:
   `benchmarks/w44_182_libjxl_cmap_dump_2026-05-21.patch`). Rebuilt
   cjxl, encoded same image with `JXL_W44_182_DUMP_CFL_CJXL=<dir>`
   set. Captured 512 dump rows (256 tiles × 2 passes). Reverted the
   libjxl patch + rebuilt to original binary (md5sum verified).
5. Diffed per-tile, computed per-pass/per-channel divergence stats
   and per-region/per-column heatmap.

Source-diff: our `find_best_multiplier_newton_*` vs libjxl `FindBestMultiplier(fast=false)`
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

`jxl-encoder-simd/src/cfl.rs:328-419` (scalar Newton, similar for AVX2/NEON/WASM128):

    pub const NEWTON_EPS_DEFAULT: f32 = 1.0;        # <-- libjxl uses 100
    pub const NEWTON_MAX_ITERS_DEFAULT: usize = 10;  # <-- libjxl uses 20

    pub fn find_best_multiplier_newton_scalar(...) -> i8 {
        # 1. Compute LS warm-start (libjxl does NOT compute warm-start)
        let ls_x = -sum_ab / (sum_aa + num as f32 * distance_mul * 0.5);
        let mut x = ls_x;        # <-- libjxl starts at x = 0
        let mut converged = false;
        for _ in 0..max_iters {
            # ... Newton iteration with eps=1 finite differences ...
            if step.abs() < NEWTON_CONVERGENCE {
                converged = true;
                break;
            }
        }
        if converged {
            bias_and_quantize(x)
        } else {
            bias_and_quantize(ls_x)   # <-- libjxl uses last `x` regardless
        }
    }

`libjxl/lib/jxl/enc_chroma_from_luma.cc:154-171`:

    constexpr float eps = 100;     // wide aperture for smoothed derivatives
    constexpr float kClamp = 20.0f;
    CFLFunction fn(values_m, values_s, num, base, distance_mul);
    x = 0;                          // start at origin
    for (size_t i = 0; i < 20; i++) {   // up to 20 iters
        float dfpeps; float dfmeps;
        float d_f = fn.Compute(x, eps, &dfpeps, &dfmeps);
        float ddf = (dfpeps - dfmeps) / (2 * eps);
        float kExperimentalInsignificantStabilizer = 0.85;
        float step = d_f / (ddf + kExperimentalInsignificantStabilizer);
        x -= std::min(kClamp, std::max(-kClamp, step));
        if (std::abs(step) < 3e-3) break;
    }
    // Whatever `x` is now, that's the answer. No fallback.

**The 4 production divergences**:

| Parameter         | Ours                  | libjxl     |
|-------------------|----------------------|------------|
| eps               | 1.0                   | 100        |
| max_iters         | 10                    | 20         |
| starting x        | ls_x (warm start)     | 0          |
| non-convergence   | fall back to ls_x     | use last x |

The "eps=1 ... convergence; libjxl uses eps=100 which causes
oscillation" docstring claim (`cfl.rs:30-34`) is FALSIFIED by direct
measurement: cjxl with eps=100 produces nonzero ytox in 134-186/256
tiles; ours with eps=1 produces 0/256.

Why eps=100 is correct (smoothed-L1 motivation)
-------------------------------------------------
The cost function is `1/3 sum((|ax+b|+1)^2 - 1) + dm*x^2*N` — a sum of
piecewise quadratic terms with kinks at each `b/a` where the absolute
value crosses zero. The exact second derivative is a sum of
delta-function-modulated quadratics; locally evaluating it via finite
differences with small `eps` produces wildly noisy values that prevent
Newton from converging. The libjxl comment EXPLICITLY says this:

    // Up to 20 Newton iterations, with approximate derivatives.
    // Derivatives are approximate due to the high amount of noise in the exact
    // derivatives.

`eps=100` smooths the second derivative over a wide aperture, making
Newton on the smoothed quasi-convex problem converge robustly to the
local minimum.

Our `eps=1` finds the LOCAL second derivative at each iteration, which
oscillates between values of opposite signs near kinks, so step length
oscillates, so step.abs() never drops below the 3e-3 convergence
threshold, so Newton fails to converge, so we fall back to `ls_x` —
which is the OLS solution (no smoothing, dominated by outlier
residuals). For X-channel CfL on smooth photo content, `ls_x` is small
(< 2.6) and gets snapped to 0 by `bias_and_quantize`.

